<h2>The three typical fact patterns</h2>
<div class="tiles">
<div class="tile"><strong>The trap case</strong> — a complainant reports a demand; the agency lays a trap; tainted currency is recovered. The evidence is front-loaded, and anticipatory bail for the person trapped is the hardest to obtain.</div>
<div class="tile"><strong>The disproportionate-assets case</strong> — built on documents: salary records, property papers, bank statements. Custodial interrogation adds little to a paper case, and pre-arrest protection with cooperation conditions is realistic.</div>
<div class="tile"><strong>The conspiracy or abetment case</strong> — private persons and co-officials roped in on statements and inference. Role attribution is contestable, and parity and delay arguments carry weight.</div>
</div>

<h2>What moves the discretion</h2>
<div class="flow">
<div class="fstep"><strong>State of the evidence</strong> — is the case already made (recovery, recordings), or does it rest on what arrest might yield? The weaker the existing material, the stronger the liberty claim — and vice versa.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Conduct on notice</strong> — compliance with Section 35 BNSS notices, attendance on summons, production of documents. Nothing persuades like a record of cooperation; nothing damns like evasion.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Flight and tampering profile</strong> — a serving officer with roots, a seized passport and departmental proceedings in motion presents a different risk than a middleman with no fixed stake.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Gravity and public trust</strong> — courts repeatedly describe corruption as an offence against society; in high-value or systemic cases, that rhetoric hardens into refusal even where the technical factors favour the applicant.</div>
</div>

<h2>Conditions that make protection workable</h2>
<p>Where courts are inclined to protect, they calibrate conditions to the investigation: joining the investigation as and when called; not contacting the complainant or co-accused; deposit of the passport; and sometimes a time-bound or chargesheet-bound operation of the order. Applicants should propose such conditions themselves — a realistic, investigation-friendly draft order often converts a wavering court.</p>

