<tr><td>Notice under s.35 BNSS received</td><td>Comply and document compliance</td><td>Arrest despite compliance requires recorded reasons</td></tr>
<tr><td>Chargesheet filed without arrest</td><td>Regular bail on appearance; Satender Antil framework</td><td>Custody is not an automatic sequel to cognizance</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="note">
<p>Corruption allegations end careers long before they reach verdict, and arrest is often the single most damaging event in the sequence. The anticipatory bail jurisprudence in this field rewards early, organised engagement: an applicant who approaches the court with the FIR analysed, cooperation demonstrated and conditions proposed stands measurably apart from one who simply pleads innocence and fears the worst. This article states the general law; it is not advice on any particular case.</p>
</div>
